window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][24] = "<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Hypothesis</th>\n<th>If true, we would also see</th>\n<th>Cheapest check</th>\n<th>Status</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Data or pipeline lag in that country</td>\n<td>Other metrics from the country (sessions, purchases) look normal; the fall disappears when data settles</td>\n<td>Second source and later data</td>\n<td>Check first</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A feature placement or promotion in that store ended</td>\n<td>A fall concentrated in a few titles that had been featured, starting on a known date</td>\n<td>The editorial calendar; downloads by title</td>\n<td>Likely</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A holiday or seasonal pattern</td>\n<td>The same fall in that country in the same week last year; across all apps</td>\n<td>Last year's week; downloads of other categories</td>\n<td>Check</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A release or policy change in the country</td>\n<td>A fall at a specific time, perhaps after a store or system change</td>\n<td>Release and policy log</td>\n<td>Check</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A network or payment disruption</td>\n<td>Failures in related flows, status reports, a fall in all downloads</td>\n<td>Status pages and error logs</td>\n<td>Check</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bot or incentivized downloads removed</td>\n<td>A fall concentrated in a few apps with unusual earlier spikes</td>\n<td>Fraud team's actions</td>\n<td>Check</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><em>Timing, location and size.</em> A candidate cause has to explain a 65,000 fall in one country starting on a date. A ended feature placement, a holiday or a disruption could each do it; the checks separate them in an hour.</p>\n<p><em>Decision.</em> If it is a calendar or featured-slot effect, no action beyond an annotation and a watch for the rebound. If it is a disruption, escalate to the owner and communicate to developers. If it is data, fix the pipeline and restate. I'd tell the store's business owner and the regional lead what we know, the size, and the next update. Prevention: alert on downloads by country, not only the global total. What would change my mind: if the fall were spread across all regions, I'd look at measurement or a global change instead.</p>\n<p><em>Company note.</em> Apple is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from third-party reports that analytical prompts at Apple include diagnosing movements in services metrics <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Apple data or system behavior is claimed.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you nest the cuts to find where the drop comes from? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I go from the largest slice downward: region, then country, then source (search, browse, ads, referrals), then device. Suppose the drop is 80,000 downloads a day and one country with 25% of volume fell 20%, which is 50,000, or 62% of the drop. Within that country, if search-driven downloads fell and browse stayed flat, that points to a ranking or search change, not a demand change. At each level I compute each slice's contribution to the total drop, so that I look first where most of it is, and I write a ledger of hypotheses with the prediction each makes, such as an outage showing an hourly dip and a campaign ending showing a drop in the ads source only.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> Who owns the decision and what do you do first? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I would name owners: the store-ranking team for search and browse changes, marketing for campaign sources, and the platform team for outages. The first action is to confirm the pattern against a second data source, then ask the owner of the most likely cause for a yes or no within 24 hours. Meanwhile I would set an interim monitor on daily downloads for the affected country, tell leadership what we know and do not know, and agree a time for the next update. A diagnosis without owners and dates tends to stall.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c13-04\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C13-04 · Intermediate · analytics · AN · Netflix</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> After a marketing campaign, sign-ups rose 25%, but 30-day retention of new members fell from 70% to 60%. Is the campaign working?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Sign-ups rose, so yes,&quot; or &quot;retention fell, so no.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Separates the campaign cohort from the others, computes the retention of the incremental members, and compares with cost.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Shows the arithmetic, treats the drop as a mix shift and not a change in the product, looks at a longer horizon, and sets a decision rule on cost per retained member.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> This is a mix question. The headline retention fell, but I don't yet know whether the product got worse or the campaign brought in a different kind of member. I know the product only from public information.</p>\n<p><em>The arithmetic.</em> Before the campaign: 100,000 sign-ups at 70% retained, so 70,000 retained at 30 days. After: 125,000 sign-ups at 60% retained, so 75,000. Suppose the 100,000 baseline sign-ups still retain at 70%. Then the 25,000 incremental sign-ups account for 75,000 − 70,000 = 5,000 retained, which is a retention of 5,000 ÷ 25,000 = 20%. The overall rate fell from 70% to 60% only because a large group retaining at 20% joined a group retaining at 70%. The rate effect on the baseline group is zero; it is a mix effect.</p>\n<p><em>What this says about the campaign.</em> It added 5,000 retained members, a 7% increase over 70,000, and its members are far less likely to stay. Whether that is a success depends on the cost. If the campaign cost $750,000 (invented), it cost $30 per sign-up and $150 per member retained at 30 days (750,000 ÷ 5,000). I would ask what a retained member is worth over their life. If it is above $150 in margin, the campaign pays; if it is below, it does not.</p>\n<p><em>What else I'd check.</em> The campaign cohort by channel, country and plan, since part may be good and part poor. A longer horizon: some members come late, and 30-day retention may understate it, so I'd look at 90-day retention for the earlier cohorts. Abuse: free-trial sign-ups that never intended to stay. And the baseline group's retention, to confirm it did not change.</p>\n<p><em>Decision.</em> Don't judge by retention alone. Judge by cost per retained member against value, and optimize targeting: shift spend to the channels where the incremental members retain better. I'd report retention for the baseline and campaign cohorts separately, so the average does not hide either. What would change my mind: if the baseline group's retention had also dropped, the problem would be the product or its early experience, not the mix.</p>\n<p><em>Company note.</em> Netflix is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports of data-informed prompts on sign-ups and retention <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Netflix data or practice is claimed.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you tell whether the product got worse or the mix changed? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Split the retained members by origin. Before: 100,000 sign-ups at 70% gives 70,000 retained. After: 125,000 at an overall 60% gives 75,000 retained. If the original 100,000 still retain at 70%, the extra 25,000 sign-ups produced only 5,000 retained members, a retention of 20%. So the product did not worsen for the baseline audience; the campaign brought a very different audience. I would confirm by splitting retention by channel and by sign-up source, and compare the campaign cohort's behavior in the first week with the baseline's.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What decision rule would you use? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Cost per incrementally retained member against its value. Suppose the campaign cost $250,000 and produced 5,000 incremental retained members: $50 per retained member. If a retained member is worth about $120 of contribution over a year, the campaign pays back at a ratio of 2.4 to 1, which may be acceptable; if the value is $40, it is not. I would also check 90-day retention of the campaign cohort, since 30-day retention can mislead, and consider onboarding changes aimed at that audience before deciding to repeat or stop the campaign.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c13-05\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C13-05 · Hard · analytics · AN · Apple</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Battery-life complaints rose after a system software update. Diagnose and decide.</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Roll back the update,&quot; or &quot;it's probably perception.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Separates complaints from measured drain, controls for who updates early, checks segments such as device age, and weighs reversibility.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Distinguishes transient from persistent effects, targets the response by device group, states a decision rule with thresholds, and sets out the communication.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I know the situation only in general terms and nothing here describes any real product. The key facts first, with assumptions: the update was released four days ago to 40% of eligible devices through a staged rollout; complaints are support contacts and feedback that mention battery.</p>\n<p><em>Is it real?</em> Complaints are a self-selected and lagging signal. People pay more attention to battery right after an update, and people who update early differ from those who wait. I'd compare complaints per 10,000 devices: updated 12, not updated 4 (invented). For the previous release's first four days, updated devices had 8 and not-updated 4, so there is a baseline gap of 4 from early adoption and attention. This release's excess over that is (12 − 4) − (8 − 4) = 4 complaints per 10,000 devices. It is a real but smaller signal than the raw ratio of 3 to 1.</p>\n<p><em>Measure drain, not only complaints.</em> From device telemetry (with the privacy limits in force), compare battery use in standard conditions between updated devices and a matched control with the same model, age and usage. Suppose the first 48 hours show drain +9% against control and by day four +2% (invented). A fast decay suggests a transient effect, for example background work after an update, which is a hypothesis to test and not a claim about any specific cause.</p>\n<p><em>Segment.</em> By device model and battery health: suppose newer devices show +1% at day four and older devices with worn batteries +6% (invented). So the effect persists for one group.</p>\n<p><em>Taxonomy.</em> Data: telemetry coverage and complaint counting. Product change: the update itself (and any app update shipped that week). External: a popular third-party app misbehaving after the change. Mix: older devices updating later. Abuse: not relevant. The evidence points at a transient effect for most and a persistent one for older devices.</p>\n<p><em>Decide (FW-C13-07).</em> Severity is moderate unless there are heat or safety signals, which would mean pause immediately. Reversibility is low: an installed update cannot easily be undone, and 60% of devices have not yet received it. So the lever is the rollout. My decision rule: if any device group shows persistent excess drain above +5% at day seven, or any thermal or safety signal appears, pause the rollout for that group. By that rule: pause the rollout for older devices, continue for newer ones, and ship a point release that addresses the persistent drain. Meanwhile set expectations for everyone updating: &quot;battery may drain faster for a day or two after updating.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Communicate.</em> Five lines for support and leadership: what (battery complaints up, excess 4 per 10,000 devices over baseline), since when (the update's release), what we know (transient for most, persistent on older devices), what we're doing (paused for older devices, point release in progress, owner named), next update in 24 hours. Prevention: add a device-age cut and a drain guardrail to the staged rollout so a pause is triggered automatically. What would change my mind: if telemetry showed drain at day four equal to control in all groups, I'd continue everywhere and treat the complaints as attention.</p>\n<p><em>Company note.</em> Apple is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from third-party reports that diagnosis prompts at Apple involve behavior after updates and the limits on reversing them <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No claim is made about any real update, telemetry or process.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you tell transient from persistent battery effects? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Use the staged rollout as a natural comparison. Compare battery telemetry for updated devices with not-yet-updated devices of the same models, by days since update. After an update, devices often run background work such as re-indexing for a day or two, which raises drain temporarily. If the difference disappears by day three, it is transient; if drain is still elevated at day seven, it is persistent. I would split by device model and age, since older batteries show effects first, and check whether complaints track the telemetry.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What would your decision rule and communication be? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;A rule written in advance: pause the rollout if updated devices show more than 10% greater battery drain than the holdout at day five in any device group, or if complaints per 1,000 updates exceed a threshold. If the effect is limited to certain models, fix and re-release for those groups first. Communication: a short, honest note saying that some devices may use more battery for a few days after updating, what to expect, and what to do, with a promised follow-up date. I would track complaints per 1,000 updates and the share of devices back to baseline by day seven.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c13-06\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C13-06 · Hard · analytics · AN · Meta</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> After a new &quot;share to story&quot; button launched, daily feed posts per user fell 6%. What do you do?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;The button is hurting feed posting, so remove it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Asks where the posting went, compares total creation, and redefines the metric.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Computes the net, considers the effect on feed supply for viewers, notes the incentive created by owning a metric for one surface, and sets a guardrail.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I know the product only from public information. Step zero: the metric counts posts to one surface, the feed. A fall in a surface metric after launching a feature on another surface is a signal to ask where the missing activity went (C13.6). Suppose feed posts per user fell from 1.00 to 0.94 a day, while story shares per user rose from 0.10 to 0.17.</p>\n<p><em>Total creation.</em> Before: 1.00 + 0.10 = 1.10 pieces of content per user. After: 0.94 + 0.17 = 1.11. Net: about +1%. People did not stop sharing; some of it moved from the feed to stories. The metric's blind spot made a flat total look like a 6% decline.</p>\n<p><em>What it does not settle.</em> Whether the move is good. Questions: did the same people shift (cannibalization), or did one group post less to the feed while another started posting to stories (users who both create and view)? Does the viewer's side change: fewer feed posts means less for viewers to see, so I'd check feed consumption and time spent, and whether stories offset it. Is story content as valuable to creators and viewers as feed content (a story disappears, a feed post persists)? And does any group, such as creators who rely on feed reach, lose?</p>\n<p><em>The incentive problem.</em> If a team is measured on feed posts per user, it may resist features that move posting elsewhere even when the company gains. This is <a href=\"#gl-goodharts-law\">Goodhart's law</a> (C05) in organizational form. The fix is to measure the outcome, total content creation and the health of both supply and consumption, and not the surface.</p>\n<p><em>Decision.</em> Don't remove the button. Redefine the creation metric as total creation across surfaces, keep feed posts per user as a diagnostic, and add guardrails: feed consumption per viewer and the share of creators whose total creation falls. Tell the owners of both surfaces what the data says. What would change my mind: if total creation had fallen, or if feed viewers' time spent fell without a matching rise in stories, the move would be a loss, and I'd revisit the design (for example, how the button is placed).</p>\n<p><em>Company note.</em> Meta is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports that prompts at Meta can involve how a metric hides a shift between surfaces and how to weigh it <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Meta data or product behavior is claimed.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you compute the net effect and what do you conclude? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;By adding both surfaces. Before: 1.00 feed posts plus 0.10 story shares is 1.10 pieces of content per user per day. After: 0.94 plus 0.17 is 1.11, a rise of under 1%. So total creation is flat and the feed metric fell because creation moved to stories. The real question is the viewers: a feed with 6% fewer posts may give viewers less to see, so I would check viewer sessions, time and satisfaction, and whether story views make up for it. The launch might be neutral or good, and a surface metric alone made it look bad.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What guardrail would you set to avoid the incentive problem? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Own a metric that spans surfaces: total content created per user and viewer satisfaction, so that a team is not rewarded for moving activity between surfaces. Add a guardrail that feed supply per viewer does not fall by more than 3% unless story consumption rises enough to offset it. Align the owners of the two surfaces on shared metrics and review them together. Otherwise each team protects its own number, and the product pays for the competition.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c13-07\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C13-07 · Hard · analytics · AN</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Overnight, daily sign-ups jumped 40%. How do you respond?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Great news; let's find out what we did right and do more of it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Treats the rise with suspicion, checks the data and the source, and looks at what the new sign-ups do afterward.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Uses post-arrival behavior to test whether they are real people, computes the implied activation, acts on the source, and protects metrics and costs.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I'd treat it as a diagnosis, with more suspicion than for a drop, because good news is accepted too easily (FW-C13-08). Step zero: a jump of this size and speed with no launch or campaign planned is unusual. Check that the data is complete and not double counted, and that the definition did not change.</p>\n<p><em>Locate.</em> Normal is 10,000 a day, today is 14,000, so 4,000 extra. By channel, country and device: suppose nearly all of the extra comes from one country and one referral source, with a narrow range of email domains and device types. A concentration like that is a sign of non-human traffic.</p>\n<p><em>Test with behavior after arrival.</em> Real people activate and return. Suppose 45% of normal sign-ups activate within a day, which on 10,000 is 4,500. Today, 4,508 activated. So the 4,000 extra sign-ups produced only 8 activations, 0.2%. The extra sign-ups did nothing, which is what bots or fake accounts do. (Activation is a <a href=\"#gl-proxy-metric\">proxy metric</a> for a real user, C05.)</p>\n<p><em>Taxonomy.</em> Abuse (family 5) fits timing, location and size. Data error is ruled out by the second source. A product or external cause would show real activation.</p>\n<p><em>Decide.</em> Severity: moderate to high. Fake accounts cost money (verification messages, storage, support), distort every metric and can harm real users if they are used for spam. Reversibility: easy for sign-up controls. Actions: block or rate-limit the source and add friction for the pattern (a challenge for sign-ups from it), remove the fake accounts from the metrics (restate the day with a note on the definition), and tell the integrity team and the owners of the dashboards. I would not report the 40% as growth.</p>\n<p><em>Communicate.</em> &quot;Sign-ups rose 40% yesterday. About 4,000 came from one source and did not activate: 8 activations against about 1,800 expected from that many real sign-ups. We believe they are fake. We are blocking the source and restating the metric. Owner: integrity. Next update at 14:00.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Prevent.</em> Monitor sign-ups by source with an activation check, and alert when a source's volume jumps while its activation is near zero. What would change my mind: if the extra sign-ups activated at a normal rate, they are real, and I'd find out what we did (a mention, a campaign) and whether it will last or is a one-day spike that pulls sign-ups forward.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you test whether the extra sign-ups are real people? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;By what they do after arriving. Normal sign-ups activate within 24 hours at about 55%; if the 4,000 extra activate at 2%, only about 80 of them are real users, and the implied activation of the whole day collapses. I would also look at email domains and patterns, device and IP clustering, sign-up timing in bursts, and whether they come through one channel. Real users show varied behavior; a bot wave shows the same steps in the same seconds. Good news gets accepted too easily, so I would be more suspicious of a jump than of a drop.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What do you do about the source, and how do you protect metrics and costs? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Block or rate-limit the source with trust and safety, and exclude the flagged accounts from reporting so that the sign-up and activation metrics are not polluted, keeping them in a separate bucket for investigation. If we pay for the traffic by sign-up, stop payment and dispute the invoice. Fix the weak point that let the wave in (a missing verification step) and add an alert for sign-up bursts by source. I would tell finance and marketing what happened and what the corrected numbers are, so that no one celebrates a number that is not real.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c13-08\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C13-08 · Beginner · analytics · AN</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> How would you count daily active users from an events table, and what can go wrong with the obvious query?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Count the rows per day.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Counts distinct users per day and says how &quot;active&quot; is defined.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> States the definition (which events, which time zone), the grain, the distinct count, and the checks (total against a known number, bots and test accounts, definition drift), and gives the query.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> <em>Definition first.</em> &quot;Active&quot; must be written down: which events count (any event, or a meaningful one), in which time zone the day is cut, and which accounts are excluded (test accounts, known bots). Without that, two people produce two numbers.</p>\n<p><em>Grain.</em> The events table has one row per user per event, so one user can have many rows in a day. The result I want has one row per day with one number: people, not events.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">SELECT event_date, COUNT(DISTINCT user_id) AS active_users\nFROM events\nWHERE event IN ('signup', 'activated')   -- whichever events define &quot;active&quot;\nGROUP BY event_date\nORDER BY event_date;\n</code></pre>\n<p><em>What goes wrong.</em> Counting rows (<code>COUNT(*)</code>) overstates users: on the practice data, 4 January has 268 rows for those two events and 241 different users. Other failures: a day boundary in the wrong time zone, which shifts activity between days; test and bot accounts that inflate the count; and a definition that changes between reports.</p>\n<p><em>Check.</em> Compare the total with a known number (the practice data has 5,000 users, so no day can have more than 5,000 active users), check the number is plausible against last week's, and read a few rows. If the number will drive a decision, get it a second way, and run the six checks of FW-C21-04 with the two added in C13.6a.</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> The definition stated first; grain; the distinct count; the failure modes; checks.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if the table has several rows per user per day and you also need weekly active users? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I'd count distinct users over the week's date range, not add the seven daily numbers, because the same person is active on several days and a sum would count them several times. Weekly active users is its own distinct count over its own window, and I'd write the window and the time zone down.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How would you tell whether a spike in the daily number is real? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I'd check the pipeline first: did the row count of the table jump, did a logging change or a new platform start emitting events, are the extra users new accounts or one device? Then I'd compare with the noise band for that day of the week (C13.3). A spike driven by a few thousand test or bot accounts would be a data issue, and I'd confirm by excluding them and recomputing.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c13-09\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C13-09 · Intermediate · analytics · AN</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Two dashboards show different week-1 retention for the same signup cohort. How do you find out why?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;One of them is wrong; I'd ask the data team.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Lists the likely causes: definition, population, window and joins.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Writes both definitions in the same four parts, reproduces both numbers with one query side by side, finds the first place they diverge, and ends with one documented definition and an owner.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> <em>Treat it as a definition problem until shown otherwise.</em> I'd write each dashboard's metric in four parts (FW-C05-01): the numerator (who counts as retained), the denominator (which users are in the cohort), the window (which days after signup) and the population (filters, such as activated users only or excluding test accounts). Differences show up in these four places, and also in the time zone and in the as-of date.</p>\n<p><em>Reproduce both with one query.</em> On the practice data, the same 5,000 users give different answers depending on the window: 745 users returned in days 7 to 13 after signup, and 107 returned in days 1 to 7.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN CAST(julianday(r.event_date) - julianday(u.signup_date) AS INT) BETWEEN 1 AND 7\n                           THEN u.user_id END) AS returned_days_1_to_7,\n       COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN CAST(julianday(r.event_date) - julianday(u.signup_date) AS INT) BETWEEN 7 AND 13\n                           THEN u.user_id END) AS returned_days_7_to_13\nFROM users u\nJOIN events r ON r.user_id = u.user_id AND r.event = 'returned';\n</code></pre>\n<p>Output: 107 and 745. Note that day 7 sits in both windows, so even the boundary needs a rule.</p>\n<p><em>Other causes to check.</em> A join that multiplies rows (fan-out, C21.4: 7,574 rows for 5,000 users on the practice data); an inner join that drops users who did nothing; a cohort that has not had its full window yet; a time-zone difference at the day boundary; one dashboard counting from a different table.</p>\n<p><em>Close it.</em> Agree one written definition with its owner, say which dashboard now follows it, and note the change date so that trend lines are not compared across the break.</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Four-part definitions; one reproducing query; the first point of divergence; documented ownership.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> Which of the two numbers is right? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Neither, until we agree what week-1 retention means for this decision. A window of days 1 to 7 measures early return and one of days 7 to 13 measures return in the second week; they answer different questions. I'd choose by the question, for example whether users come back after the first week, then name the definition in the dashboard title so the number cannot be misread.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if the two dashboards disagree only slightly? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Then I'd look for the smaller causes: a time-zone cut, a filter for test accounts, a population of activated users versus all signups, or rounding. A small gap with a clear cause is still worth closing, because it will grow when the data changes. I'd also check whether the gap is within the noise of a sample, if one of them is built on a sample.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c13-10\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C13-10 · Hard · analytics · AN</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Describe, in SQL or in words, how you would compute week-1 retention by signup week and channel, and say what could mislead you when you read it.</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Group users by week and channel and count who came back.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Gives the right grain and joins, and notes small cohorts.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Builds a per-user flag first, groups after, defines the window, handles incomplete cohorts, reads rates with sample sizes and noise, and names the mix and survivorship traps.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> <em>Per-user flag first, group after.</em> I'd build one row per user with a retention flag, then aggregate. That fixes the grain and avoids fan-out from the events table.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">WITH base AS (\n  SELECT u.user_id,\n         strftime('%Y-%W', u.signup_date) AS signup_week,\n         u.channel,\n         MAX(CASE WHEN e.event = 'returned'\n                   AND CAST(julianday(e.event_date) - julianday(u.signup_date) AS INT) BETWEEN 7 AND 13\n                  THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS returned\n  FROM users u\n  LEFT JOIN events e ON e.user_id = u.user_id\n  GROUP BY u.user_id)\nSELECT signup_week, channel, COUNT(*) AS signups, SUM(returned) AS returned,\n       ROUND(100.0 * SUM(returned) / COUNT(*), 1) AS return_pct\nFROM base GROUP BY signup_week, channel ORDER BY signup_week, channel;\n</code></pre>\n<p>On the practice data the output is, for signup weeks 2027-01, 2027-02 and 2027-03: organic 18.0%, 14.0% and 16.5% (771, 713 and 764 signups); paid 9.3%, 6.9% and 9.0% (592, 567 and 586); referral 24.2%, 19.7% and 25.6% (335, 340 and 332).</p>\n<p><em>What could mislead.</em> (1) <em>Incomplete cohorts:</em> the latest week may not have had the full window yet, so its rate is understated; I'd state the as-of date and drop cohorts that are not complete. (2) <em>Small cells and noise:</em> a channel-week with 335 users has a wide margin (several points), and the middle week here is lower in all three channels even though the data were built with the same return probability each week; I'd check the noise band before explaining a difference (C13.3). (3) <em>Mix:</em> an overall retention number blends channels, so a rise in paid signups lowers it with no change inside any channel (Worked example 7); report by channel. (4) <em>Survivorship:</em> if I restrict to users who activated, I answer a different question; I'd say which population I mean. (5) <em>Definition drift:</em> the window and the event must match the dashboard's definition. (6) <em>Many comparisons:</em> nine cells will show a few differences by chance.</p>\n<p><em>Check.</em> The cells' signups should add to 5,000, the rates should lie between 0 and 100, and a second method (a pivot table on a sample) should agree on at least one cell (the checks of FW-C21-04 and C13.6a).</p>\n<p><em>Signals.</em> Per-user flag then group; the window; incomplete cohorts; noise; mix; survivorship; checks.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How would you handle the most recent signup week? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I'd set an as-of date and include a cohort only if every user in it has had the full window, so the last incomplete week is left out or shown separately and marked as partial. Otherwise it will look like a drop in retention when it is only a shorter observation period. I'd also show the count of users who have completed the window so the reader sees how much of the cohort is in.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> Paid retention looks lower than organic in every week. Is that a finding? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;It is a consistent difference, with n in the hundreds for each cell, so it is more than one noisy week. Before calling it a finding I'd check that the definition and population are the same across channels, whether paid users differ in how they were acquired (for example, they were incentivized), and what the difference costs: lower retention is acceptable if the acquisition cost is low enough (C20.7). I'd report the rate with its sample size and a range.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c13-12-exercises\"></span>\n<h3>12. Exercises</h3>\n<p>Ten exercises. Each has a model solution and an alternative. Figures are assumptions unless marked, and the arithmetic was computed.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c13-01\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C13-01 · Is the data complete?</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> At 9:00 on Tuesday the dashboard shows 3,000 orders so far today, against 5,000 at 9:00 last Tuesday: −40%. Events reach the dashboard with a delay of up to 90 minutes. At 7:30 last Tuesday the dashboard had shown 3,100 orders. What do you conclude?</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> You are asked whether to page the on-call team.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Three minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> The step-zero card (FW-C13-02): compare like with like on completeness.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> With a 90-minute delay, the 9:00 dashboard contains events only up to about 7:30. The like-for-like comparison is today's 3,000 against 3,100 at 7:30 last Tuesday: −3.2%, which is within normal variation. The −40% compares different amounts of data. Do not page; check again at 11:00 when the data has settled, and say that you would.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Compare today's complete 7:30 count from the raw event stream, if available, with last Tuesday's.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Waiting costs time if something is truly wrong; a second quick source (payment success, support contacts) can check it meanwhile.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c13-02\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C13-02 · The noise band</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Daily orders (thousands) for eight comparable days: 10.2, 9.8, 10.1, 10.4, 9.9, 10.0, 10.3, 9.7. Today is 9.5. Compute the mean and the standard deviation, and say whether today is unusual.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> A team lead asks whether to investigate.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Four minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> The noise band (C12.3, C13.3).</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> The mean is 10.05 and the standard deviation is about 0.245, or 2.4% of the mean. Today's 9.5 is 0.55 below the mean, which is 5.5% and about 2.25 standard deviations. It is unusual but with eight days of history it is borderline: about one day in forty falls this far below by chance. A reasonable response is a ten-minute check of segments and the calendar, not a full investigation, and a rule to escalate if tomorrow is also low.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Use the same weekday for the baseline if orders vary by weekday, which may make the band tighter and today more unusual.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A short history gives an uncertain band; watching many metrics yields occasional false alarms.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c13-03\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C13-03 · Contributions to a drop</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Sign-ups by channel, before and after: paid search 3,000 to 2,950; social 2,000 to 1,990; referral 1,500 to 1,200; organic 3,500 to 3,360. Compute each channel's absolute contribution to the change, its share of the drop and its relative change. Which do you investigate first?</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Total sign-ups fell from 10,000 to 9,500.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Contribution analysis (C13.5, FW-C13-04).</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Contributions: paid −50 (10% of the drop), social −10 (2%), referral −300 (60%), organic −140 (28%); total −500. Relative changes: paid −1.7%, social −0.5%, referral −20%, organic −4%. Referral explains the most and fell the most in relative terms: investigate first. Organic is the second: 28% of the drop with a small relative fall, because it is a large channel; it may be a seasonal or search-ranking effect, and should not be skipped.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Check whether referral and organic share a cause (for example, a change to a sharing feature that also affects organic discovery).</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Reading only relative changes would send you to referral and miss that organic is nearly a third of the drop.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c13-04\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C13-04 · Rate effect and mix effect</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Activation rate by acquisition channel: before, channel A is 60% of sign-ups at a 50% activation rate and channel B is 40% at 30%; after, A is 40% at 49% and B is 60% at 31%. Compute the overall rates, the change, the rate effect and the mix effect.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Leadership asks why activation fell.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C13-04.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Before: 0.6 × 50 + 0.4 × 30 = 42.0%. After: 0.4 × 49 + 0.6 × 31 = 38.2%. Change: −3.8 points. Rate effect (old shares, rate changes): 0.6 × (49 − 50) + 0.4 × (31 − 30) = −0.6 + 0.4 = −0.2. Mix effect (share changes at the new rates): (0.4 − 0.6) × 49 + (0.6 − 0.4) × 31 = −9.8 + 6.2 = −3.6. Total −3.8. Within each channel activation barely moved; the fall is a mix shift toward the lower-activating channel B. The question becomes whether the growth in B is wanted and whether B's activation can be improved.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Report activation by channel and a mix-adjusted overall figure (at the old mix: 0.6 × 49 + 0.4 × 31 = 41.8%).</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A mix shift can be good (more users in a harder channel); judge it by cost and lifetime value, not by the average.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c13-05\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C13-05 · Find the funnel step</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A sign-up funnel: 100,000 visits; 30,000 start sign-up (30%); 18,000 complete the form (60% of starts); 12,600 verify email (70% of completes); 7,560 take a first action (60% of verified). After a change: 100,000; 30,000; 18,000; 9,900 verify (55%); 5,940 first action (60%). Which step changed, how many users were lost there, and what is the effect on the end metric? What would you check?</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> First actions fell from 7,560 to 5,940.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> The funnel decomposition (FW-C13-05).</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> The email verification step fell from 70% to 55%; other rates are unchanged. Users lost there: 12,600 − 9,900 = 2,700. They would have taken a first action at 60%, so the end metric lost 2,700 × 0.6 = 1,620, matching 7,560 − 5,940. The relative effect is 0.55 ÷ 0.70 = 0.786, a 21% fall at the end. What I'd check: whether the verification email is delivered (spam folder, a changed sender or template), how long it takes to arrive, whether the link works on mobile, and whether the verification step itself changed.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Compare by email provider: if the fall is concentrated in one provider's users, it points to deliverability.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Fixing deliverability is fast; changing the step design is slower and may be less necessary.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c13-06\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C13-06 · A hypothesis ledger</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Orders fell 20% starting at 15:00 on Tuesday, across all platforms. No release shipped that day. Build a ledger for four hypotheses: (a) a payment provider incident, (b) a release or configuration change, (c) a holiday or external event, (d) a data pipeline or definition change. For each, give a prediction, the cheapest check and what it would rule out.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> You have ten minutes before a leadership update.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Seven minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C13-06; test timing, location and size.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> (a) Payment incident: payment success rate falls at 15:00, orders fall with it, error codes from the provider rise, the provider's status page shows an incident; check payment success by method and the provider's status. Rules out if the success rate is flat. (b) Release or configuration: a change logged near 15:00 (a flag, a config, a campaign); check the change log and flags; rules out if nothing changed. (c) External: the fall matches a regional event or outage, and other companies' services show similar patterns; check the calendar and status pages; rules out if the pattern is not across the market. (d) Data: other metrics do not fall (payments received, support contacts), and the dashboard disagrees with the source of truth; check a second source; rules out if the money did not move. Order: (d), then (b), then (a), then (c).</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Start with (a) if a payment alert fired at 15:00, since the first strong evidence sets the order.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A fixed order is efficient on average; one strong early signal should override it.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c13-07\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C13-07 · Fix, roll back, pause or monitor?</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Choose and justify an action: (a) a checkout bug affects 5% of users, and a fix will be ready in two hours; (b) a metric is 1.5% below normal, inside the usual swing; (c) a software update, already installed on 40% of devices, shows a persistent fault on one model.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Use severity, reversibility and the cost of waiting.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> The matrix in C13.8 and FW-C13-07.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> (a) High severity (revenue) and easy to reverse: roll back the release that introduced the bug if that is faster than two hours; otherwise switch off the affected path with a flag, then fix; the cost of waiting is 2 hours of failed checkouts for 5% of users. (b) Low severity and noise: monitor with a threshold (investigate if it persists for two days or exceeds the noise band). (c) High severity for those users and hard to reverse: pause the rollout for that model, fix forward quickly, communicate to affected users, and do not try to recall what is installed.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> In (a), if the rollback would also remove an important unrelated fix, use the flag.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Rolling back is cheap when it is possible; a rollback that is itself risky (a data migration) changes the answer.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c13-08\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C13-08 · Pull-forward</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Weekly sales are normally 100 (index). A promotion week reaches 125. The next two weeks are 80 and 90. What is the net effect over three weeks, and how do you report it?</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> The marketing team wants to report a 25% lift.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Four minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> The good-news check (FW-C13-08).</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Over three weeks sales total 125 + 80 + 90 = 295 against a baseline of 300: a net change of −5, or −1.7%. The promotion moved demand forward and did not create it; the weeks after fell below baseline by 30. Report the three-week (or longer) net with the weekly pattern, and if the promotion had a cost, report the net after the cost. The right comparison is with a holdout group that did not get the promotion, if possible.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> If some promoted customers are new and stay, the after-weeks may recover; extend the window to six weeks before judging.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A longer window delays the answer and gives a truer one.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c13-09\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C13-09 · The five-line message</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Write the status message: sign-ups fell 20% on Tuesday; the cause is a form release at 08:10 that broke sign-up on older versions of one browser; about 1,960 sign-ups were lost; the release is being rolled back, finishing at about 09:40; web engineering owns the fix.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> The message goes to leadership and support at 09:10.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five lines.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> The template in FW-C13-07.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> <em>What:</em> Sign-ups fell 20% on Tuesday (8,000 against about 10,000); one browser accounts for 98% of the drop. <em>Since when:</em> 08:10 Tuesday, when the new sign-up form was released. <em>What we know and don't:</em> The form breaks sign-up on older versions of that browser, about 1,960 sign-ups lost; we do not yet know whether any users who hit the error will return. <em>What we're doing and who owns it:</em> Rolling back the form release now, owner web engineering, completion expected around 09:40; support can tell affected users to use another browser. <em>Next update:</em> 11:00, with recovery numbers.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Add one line on prevention (an alert on sign-up conversion by browser) in the follow-up message.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A longer message adds detail and delays the key facts; lead with what and the action.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c13-10\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C13-10 · A timed full diagnosis</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Give a 20 to 25 minute answer: &quot;Active paying subscribers fell 2% month over month. Why, and what do you do?&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Self-scored; record yourself.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None. Ask for what you need and say what you would expect.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Use the 30-minute budget in section 4, compressed.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> The full spine (FW-C13-01), scored against the six behaviors in section 1.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> An outline of a strong answer. Step zero: active subscribers is a stock, so decompose it into flows: gross additions, cancellations, reactivations and involuntary churn (failed payments). Confirm the definition, the window (month length, billing dates), and completeness. Locate: which flow moved? Gross additions down (acquisition), cancellations up (retention), payment failures up (billing).</p>\n<p>Then segment the flow that moved by plan, channel, cohort and country. Taxonomy: data (a billing system change that miscounts), product (a price change, a removed feature, a billing flow change), external (seasonality such as a holiday month, competitor), mix (more annual plans changing the timing of cancellations), abuse (fraudulent trials ending). Test each against timing, location and size. Decide according to which flow: if payment failures, fix the billing retry logic (reversible and urgent); if a price change, consider a rollback or grandfathering; if seasonality, monitor. Communicate in five lines and add an alert on each flow.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Start with the flows from a subscription model and ask which of gross adds or churn moved, which narrows the search before any hypotheses.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A stock metric moves slowly and hides which flow changed; decomposing into flows is the first move.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c13-11\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C13-11 · The 100% activation rate</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A report shows activation of 100% in every acquisition channel, which nobody believes. The query joins <code>users</code> to <code>events</code> with <code>JOIN events a ON a.user_id = u.user_id AND a.event = 'activated'</code> and groups by channel. Find the error, give the right query and say how you would have caught it.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> A funnel by segment in a diagnosis (C13.5, C13.6).</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> The practice dataset (Appendix B, script 1; invented). The query as written returns, for organic, paid and referral, 1,208 users with 1,208 activated, 699 with 699, and 667 with 667.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C21-04 and C13.6a.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> The inner <code>JOIN</code> keeps only users who have an <code>activated</code> event, so every user in the result has activated by construction and the users who did not activate, the rest of the denominator, are gone. The rate is activated users divided by activated users. The right query keeps all users with a <code>LEFT JOIN</code> and puts the event condition in the <code>ON</code> clause: <code>FROM users u LEFT JOIN events a ON a.user_id = u.user_id AND a.event = 'activated' GROUP BY u.channel</code>, counting <code>DISTINCT u.user_id</code> for signups and <code>DISTINCT a.user_id</code> for activated. On the practice data it returns organic 2,248 signups and 1,208 activated (53.7%), paid 1,745 and 699 (40.1%), referral 1,007 and 667 (66.2%). The check that catches it is the total against a known number: the wrong result's users sum to 2,574 (1,208 + 699 + 667), but the table has 5,000 users; the right result's signups sum to 5,000.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Compute a per-user flag first (<code>MAX(CASE WHEN event = 'activated' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END)</code> grouped by user) and then average the flag by channel; the denominator is every user by construction.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A <code>LEFT JOIN</code> with a condition in the <code>ON</code> clause is easy to get wrong (moving the condition to <code>WHERE</code> turns it back into an inner join); the per-user flag is longer and harder to misread.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c13-12\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C13-12 · Write the funnel query</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> The <code>events</code> table (<code>user_id</code>, <code>event</code>, <code>event_date</code>) has three events: <code>visit</code>, <code>add_to_cart</code> and <code>purchase</code>. Write one query that returns the number of distinct users at each step, then compute the step-to-step and overall conversion. Some users have several <code>add_to_cart</code> rows.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> A funnel question in a diagnosis (C13.6).</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> Run on a test table built to these numbers (invented; Appendix B, script 3): 10,000 users visited, 2,400 added to cart, 600 purchased; 300 of the users who added to cart did so on two different days.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Eight minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> The funnel pattern in C13.6a, FW-C13-05 and FW-C21-04.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN event='visit'       THEN user_id END) AS visited,\n       COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN event='add_to_cart' THEN user_id END) AS added,\n       COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN event='purchase'    THEN user_id END) AS purchased\nFROM events;\n</code></pre>\n<p>Output on the test table: 10,000; 2,400; 600. Conversion: visit to cart 2,400 ÷ 10,000 = 24%; cart to purchase 600 ÷ 2,400 = 25%; overall 600 ÷ 10,000 = 6%. The repeated rows matter: counting <code>add_to_cart</code> rows instead of users gives 2,700 (2,400 users plus 300 repeat rows), which would show a cart rate of 27% and a cart-to-purchase rate of 22%. The distinct count answers &quot;how many people&quot;, which is the funnel's question. This funnel assumes the steps are nested (every purchaser also visited); check that the counts of later steps do not exceed earlier ones.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Build a per-user table with one flag per step and count the flags; it makes the nesting check explicit (<code>purchase = 1 AND visit = 0</code> should return no rows).</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A funnel over all time mixes users at different stages; for a diagnosis, restrict to a signup or visit window and state it.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c13-13\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C13-13 · Two dashboards, one cohort</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Dashboard A reports 107 users returned in week 1 and dashboard B reports 745, for the same 5,000 signups. List the four questions you would ask, write one query that shows both numbers side by side, and say what you would do next.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> The scenario in IQ-C13-09, on the practice data.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> The practice dataset from Appendix B, script 1 (invented). Dashboard A uses days 1 to 7 after signup; dashboard B uses days 7 to 13.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Eight minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C05-01 and FW-C21-04.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> <em>Four questions:</em> what counts as retained (the numerator), who is in the cohort (the denominator), which days after signup (the window, and whether day 7 is included), and which filters apply (the population, time zone and as-of date). <em>One query:</em></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN CAST(julianday(r.event_date) - julianday(u.signup_date) AS INT) BETWEEN 1 AND 7\n                           THEN u.user_id END) AS returned_days_1_to_7,\n       COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN CAST(julianday(r.event_date) - julianday(u.signup_date) AS INT) BETWEEN 7 AND 13\n                           THEN u.user_id END) AS returned_days_7_to_13\nFROM users u\nJOIN events r ON r.user_id = u.user_id AND r.event = 'returned';\n</code></pre>\n<p>Output: 107 and 745. The windows differ, and day 7 appears in both. <em>Next:</em> choose the definition by the question the metric answers, write it as numerator, denominator, window and population, assign an owner, change the other dashboard to match (or rename it) and record the date of the change so that trends are not compared across it.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Report both windows on one dashboard with clear names (&quot;returned in days 1 to 7&quot; and &quot;returned in days 7 to 13&quot;) if both answer real questions.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> One definition is simpler to read; two named definitions preserve information when they answer different questions.</p>\n<span id=\"c13-13-practice-protocol\"></span>\n<h3>13. Practice protocol</h3>\n<p><strong>Timed drills (three a week for four weeks).</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Week 1: step zero and the noise band.</em> Use IQ-C13-01, EX-C13-01 and EX-C13-02. Run the six questions in two minutes and say a verdict. Compute a mean and standard deviation by hand.</li>\n<li><em>Week 2: locate.</em> Use IQ-C13-02, IQ-C13-03, EX-C13-03, EX-C13-04 and EX-C13-05. Do contribution, rate-and-mix and funnel arithmetic by hand, and say aloud where the change sits and why. For the SQL checks (C13.6a) add IQ-C13-08, EX-C13-11 and EX-C13-12.</li>\n<li><em>Week 3: hypotheses and decisions.</em> Use IQ-C13-04, IQ-C13-05, IQ-C13-06, EX-C13-06, EX-C13-07 and EX-C13-09. Build a ledger with predictions, choose an action by severity and reversibility, and write the five-line message. For the SQL checks add IQ-C13-09, IQ-C13-10 and EX-C13-13 (run the queries on the Appendix B data).</li>\n<li><em>Week 4: full diagnoses.</em> Use IQ-C13-07 and EX-C13-10, plus two prompts you have not seen. Run two 25-minute answers and one 10-minute short version, with a partner who uses the interventions in section 4 and changes the facts partway.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>With a partner.</strong> Ask your partner to hold the data and reveal it only when you ask a specific question, and to give a surprising result at the second or third check. Practice updating the ledger aloud when the evidence changes.</p>\n<p><strong>Keep a diagnosis log.</strong> For each drill, record the order of your checks and which one found the answer. Over eight drills, see whether you check measurement first and whether your cuts are in a planned order.</p>\n<p><strong>Feedback checklist (yes, partly, no).</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Did I confirm the change was real before explaining it?</li>\n<li>Did I compare with a baseline and the normal swing, not only yesterday?</li>\n<li>Did I check the cheap and common families first, in a stated order?</li>\n<li>Did I locate the change with segments and funnel steps, and quantify the shares?</li>\n<li>Did my hypotheses predict something I could check, and did I rule some out?</li>\n<li>Did the cause match on timing, location and size?</li>\n<li>Did I split rate and mix effects for an average or a rate?</li>\n<li>Did I decide with severity, reversibility and the cost of waiting in view?</li>\n<li>Did I give a five-line message and one preventive step?</li>\n<li>Did I finish with the two-sentence close from section 4?</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer journal.</strong> For each drill record the date, the prompt, the cause found, the check that found it, your score out of ten and the step you rushed. After eight drills, look for the step you skip most (usually the confirmation or the communication) and drill it alone for a week.</p>\n<span id=\"c13-14-implementation-playbook\"></span>\n<h3>14. Implementation playbook</h3>\n<p>Apply the chapter on real work over the next three weeks. Each deliverable doubles as an interview story.</p>\n<p><strong>Days 1 to 3.</strong> For the main metric you own, write down its definition, its normal day-to-day swing, its weekday and seasonal pattern, and how long its data takes to settle. Share it with your manager and the data team. This is your step-zero card.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 1.</strong> Look at the last real change in that metric (a dip or a rise). Without looking at what was concluded at the time, run the spine on it. Compare with what the team found, and note which checks would have been faster.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 1.</strong> Ask engineering for the release log, flag changes and running experiments for the last month, and overlay them on your metric. Mark where changes line up with movements.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 2.</strong> Build a segmentation sweep for your metric: the table of cuts in order with contributions (FW-C13-04). Make it reusable, so the next time the metric moves it takes ten minutes. Ask the data team to automate it if it is valuable.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 2.</strong> Set one alert that would have caught a past problem sooner: the metric by platform or by country, or a funnel step. Write the threshold and who gets paged.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 3.</strong> Write a runbook of one page: the first fifteen minutes when the metric moves (the step-zero questions, the taxonomy order, who to ask, the five-line message template). Test it on a drill with a teammate.</p>\n<p><strong>Deliverables that become stories:</strong> the retrospective diagnosis (a time you reasoned your way to a cause in order), the segmentation sweep and alert (a time you shortened the next diagnosis), and the runbook (a time you made a team faster). Keep the numbers real, and keep what you did separate from what the team did.</p>\n<span id=\"c13-15-key-takeaways-and-further-reading\"></span>\n<h3>15. Key takeaways and further reading</h3>\n<span id=\"c13-key-takeaways\"></span>\n<h4>Key takeaways</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>Confirm the change is real before explaining it: check the definition, the baseline and normal swing, whether the data is complete, and a second source.</li>\n<li>Work through causes in a stated order, cheapest and most common first: data and instrumentation, our own changes, external events, mix shift, abuse, and a real change in behavior by elimination.</li>\n<li>Locate the change with segments and funnel steps, quantify each one's share, and split an average or rate into a rate effect and a mix effect.</li>\n<li>Make each hypothesis predict something you can check, and accept a cause only if it explains timing, location and size.</li>\n<li>Decide by severity, reversibility and the cost of waiting, send a five-line message with a next-update time, and treat a rise with the same suspicion as a drop.</li>\n</ol>\n<span id=\"c13-further-reading\"></span>\n<h4>Further reading</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Betsy Beyer, Chris Jones, Jennifer Petoff and Niall Richard Murphy (eds.), <em>Site Reliability Engineering</em> (2016), including the chapters on incident response and postmortem culture. Public practice for diagnosing and learning from failures <code>[V: Beyer, Jones, Petoff and Murphy, Site Reliability Engineering, O'Reilly, 2016]</code>.</li>\n<li>Ron Kohavi, Diane Tang and Ya Xu, <em>Trustworthy Online Controlled Experiments</em> (2020). Includes Twyman's law and the checks that catch bad data.</li>\n<li>Sidney Dekker, <em>The Field Guide to Understanding 'Human Error'</em> (2006). On investigating without blame <code>[V: 2nd edition, Ashgate, 2006, ISBN 9780754648260; revises The Field Guide to Human Error Investigations (2002)]</code>.</li>\n<li>Alistair Croll and Benjamin Yoskovitz, <em>Lean Analytics</em> (2013). Choosing and reading metrics at each stage <code>[V: O'Reilly, 2013, ISBN 9781449335670]</code>.</li>\n<li>Donella H. Meadows, <em>Thinking in Systems</em> (2008). Feedback and delays, which explain many surprising metric movements.</li>\n<li>Marshall Miller, &quot;New User Trends on Wikipedia,&quot; Wikimedia Foundation Diff blog, 17 October 2025 (diff.wikimedia.org/2025/10/17/new-user-trends-on-wikipedia/). The primary source for CS-C13-02.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c14\"></span>\n<h2>C14 — Experiment Design and Causal Reasoning</h2>\n<span id=\"c14-1-what-interviewers-test\"></span>\n<h3>1. What interviewers test</h3>\n<p>&quot;How would you test this?&quot; and &quot;The test shows a lift; do we ship?&quot; are standard product questions, and the best candidates answer them the same way: they decide what decision the test serves, say who is randomized and what is measured, and say in advance what result would lead to what action. Weaker answers say &quot;run an A/B test&quot; and stop. Another common weak answer is to compare numbers before and after a launch and call the difference the effect of the launch.</p>\n<p><strong>Where it shows up.</strong> <code>Inferred</code> from the round structures candidates commonly report: experiment design and interpretation questions appear in analytical and execution rounds at Google, Meta, Microsoft, Amazon, Apple, Netflix and Stripe, with the heaviest emphasis at companies known to run very large numbers of online tests <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. On the job it is a PM's regular work: deciding what to test, defining success before the data arrive, reading a readout and saying ship, hold or roll back.</p>\n<p><strong>What is scored.</strong> Causal reasoning and judgment, not formulas <code>Inferred</code>. Interviewers listen for whether you separate correlation from effect, whether you randomize at a sensible unit, whether you name a primary metric, guardrails and a decision rule before seeing results, whether you size the test and know the usual threats to a valid result, and whether you have something sensible to say when randomization is not possible.</p>\n<p>Six observable behaviors:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>You state the decision, the hypothesis and what you would do under each outcome before designing the test.</li>\n<li>You choose the unit of randomization with a reason and analyze at that unit.</li>\n<li>You name one primary metric, the guardrails and the decision rule in advance.</li>\n<li>You size the test (effect worth detecting, sample, duration) and say what you would do if it is too long.</li>\n<li>You name the validity threats that matter here (sample-ratio mismatch, novelty, interference, peeking) and how you would check them.</li>\n<li>You say what you would do when you cannot randomize, and how much weaker the evidence is.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>The failure this prevents.</strong> Six patterns end experiment answers: &quot;run an A/B test&quot; with no design, a before-and-after comparison presented as an effect, a long list of metrics with no primary, no decision rule, trusting a result without checking that the groups were split as planned, and shipping on the first day the result turns significant.</p>\n<span id=\"c14-2-the-concept-from-first-principles\"></span>\n<h3>2. The concept from first principles</h3>\n<span id=\"c14-c14-1-causation-versus-correlation-the-counterfactual\"></span>\n<h4>C14.1 Causation versus correlation; the counterfactual</h4>\n<p>A <strong>causal effect</strong> is the difference in outcome caused by a change, compared with what would have happened without it. The comparison is with a <strong>counterfactual</strong>: what would have happened to the same units if they had not received the change. The difficulty is that you never see both for the same unit. A customer either attended the webinar or did not; you observe one world.</p>\n<p><strong>Why simple comparisons mislead.</strong> Compare people who got the change with people who did not, and you are comparing two groups that may differ for reasons unrelated to the change. A <strong>confounder</strong> is a factor linked to both a change and an outcome that makes the observed relationship misleading. Customers who choose to attend an onboarding webinar are more motivated and more likely to renew regardless of the webinar; &quot;webinar attendees renew more&quot; mixes the effect of the webinar with the effect of motivation. Other traps from C12 apply: survivorship (<a href=\"#gl-survivorship-bias\">survivorship bias</a>) and regression to the mean (<a href=\"#gl-regression-to-the-mean\">regression to the mean</a>).</p>\n<p><strong>Randomization.</strong> <strong>Randomization</strong> is assigning units to variants by chance so that the groups differ, on average, only in the treatment. If a coin flip decides who gets the change, motivation, device, country and every other factor, measured or not, are balanced across the groups on average, and a difference in outcomes can be attributed to the change. An <strong>experiment</strong> is a controlled test in which units are randomly assigned to variants so the effect of a change can be measured. An <strong>A/B test</strong> is an experiment with two groups: users are randomly assigned to the current version (A) or the changed version (B), and the same metric is compared between them. <a href=\"#gl-a-b-test\">A/B test</a> is the form most product experiments take.</p>\n<p><strong>A ladder of evidence (FW-C14-02).</strong> From weakest to strongest: an anecdote; a correlation in the data; a before-and-after comparison (confounded by anything else that changed over time); a comparison with a control group chosen by the analyst (confounded by how it was chosen); a comparison with a matched or modeled control; a randomized experiment. Each step rules out more explanations. Say which rung you are on and what it cannot rule out.</p>\n<p><strong>When not to run an experiment.</strong> When the effect is obvious and the change is a fix (a crash on one browser needs no test); when randomizing would be unsafe or unethical (withholding a safety warning); when the traffic is too small to detect any worthwhile effect; when the change is cheap and reversible and you can learn faster by shipping and watching; and when the question is &quot;why&quot; and not &quot;how much&quot;, for which research (C06) is the tool.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Counterexample.</strong> A team sees that customers who attended its onboarding webinars renew at 92%, against 78% for those who did not. It concludes that webinars cause renewals and requires every new customer to attend. A year later, renewals are unchanged and complaints about the forced webinar have risen. The gap was a confounder: the customers who chose to attend were the engaged ones. A randomized invitation, where half of new customers were invited and half were not, would have shown a much smaller effect, perhaps none, and cost far less than a company-wide mandate.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Hidden risks.</strong> Two. <em>Treating the experiment as magic:</em> a randomized test can still be invalid (a broken split, a wrong unit, a short window), and a small, noisy result is still noisy (C12). <em>Treating the result as a law:</em> an experiment measures what happened to these users in this period; whether it generalizes to other users, other seasons or the long run is a judgment.</p>\n<span id=\"c14-c14-2-anatomy-of-an-experiment-unit-randomization-treatment-\"></span>\n<h4>C14.2 Anatomy of an experiment: unit, randomization, treatment, metrics</h4>\n<p>FW-C14-01 is a one-page design card with the parts below.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Part</th>\n<th>The question</th>\n<th>Notes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Decision and hypothesis</td>\n<td>What will we do differently depending on the result? What do we believe and why?</td>\n<td>A test with no decision is research; fine, but say so</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Unit</td>\n<td>Who or what is randomized: user, account, device, session, store, city, a cluster of friends?</td>\n<td>Pick the unit at which the treatment is experienced and at which outcomes should be independent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Randomization</td>\n<td>How is assignment made?</td>\n<td>A stable hash of the unit ID with an experiment-specific salt, so a unit always sees the same variant</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Treatment and control</td>\n<td>What exactly differs?</td>\n<td>Keep the control the current experience; one change per test where possible</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Exposure</td>\n<td>Who actually could be affected?</td>\n<td>Analyze those who reached the changed feature (&quot;triggered&quot; analysis) to avoid dilution</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Metrics</td>\n<td>One primary, a few guardrails, some secondary</td>\n<td>Decided in advance (C05, FW-C05-06)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Analysis</td>\n<td>What comparison, at what unit, with what interval?</td>\n<td>Analyze as assigned (intent to treat), at the unit of randomization</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Duration</td>\n<td>How long, in whole weeks?</td>\n<td>Set from the sample size (C14.3) and the need to cover weekly cycles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Decision rule</td>\n<td>What result means ship, hold, roll back?</td>\n<td>Written before the data arrive (C14.5)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>The unit.</strong> Randomize at the level at which the change is experienced and at which behavior is independent. For a page layout, the user (so a person does not see different layouts on different visits). For a pricing change, perhaps the account or the market. If the unit of randomization is the user but you analyze at the level of sessions or page views, the observations are not independent and your intervals are too narrow (C12.3).</p>\n<p><strong>Consistency.</strong> A unit should see one variant throughout. If a person is assigned by cookie and uses two devices, they may see both, which contaminates the comparison; assign by a logged-in identifier where there is one.</p>\n<p><strong>A/A tests.</strong> Running a test in which both arms are identical checks the machinery: about 5% of such tests should show a significant difference by chance, and a much higher rate signals a faulty setup. Teams use A/A tests to validate a new experimentation system.</p>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> The card forces the decisions that are otherwise made implicitly and wrongly. <strong>When not to fill in every row.</strong> For a trivial, reversible change, the unit, the primary metric and the decision rule are enough.</p>\n<span id=\"c14-c14-3-power-minimum-detectable-effect-duration-and-sample-si\"></span>\n<h4>C14.3 Power, minimum detectable effect, duration and sample size at PM depth</h4>\n<p>C12.4 introduced power and the rule of 16. Here is how it is used in design. The <strong>minimum detectable effect</strong> is the smallest true effect a test is designed to detect with stated power and significance. It should come from the decision: the smallest effect that would justify shipping given its cost (C12.5), not the smallest effect the traffic can reveal.</p>\n<p><strong>The planner (FW-C14-04).</strong> (1) Write the baseline rate and the smallest worthwhile effect. (2) Users per arm ≈ 16 × p(1 − p) ÷ δ² for 80% power at 5% significance. (3) Divide by the daily eligible users per arm to get days, and round up to whole weeks so that every weekday appears equally in both arms. (4) If it is too long, raise the minimum effect if the decision allows, narrow to the people the change can affect, choose a more sensitive metric, accept lower power with a staged rollout, or decide without a test.</p>\n<p><strong>Dilution.</strong> The effect you can detect depends on who is in the analysis. If a feature is seen by only 10% of users, and the metric is something every user has (say, a 20% weekly purchase rate), a +2 point effect among those who see it is only +0.2 points across everyone. Analyzing all users needs 16 × 0.16 ÷ 0.002² = 640,000 users per arm. Analyzing only the exposed needs 16 × 0.16 ÷ 0.02² = 6,400 exposed users per arm, which at 10% exposure is 64,000 users per arm of traffic. The triggered analysis needs a tenth of the traffic. The cost: you must log exposure correctly and trigger identically in both arms (otherwise you create a sample-ratio problem, C14.4).</p>\n<p><strong>Other design choices.</strong> <em>Split:</em> 50/50 maximizes power for two arms; unequal splits (90/10) cost power and are used for safety. <em>Number of variants:</em> each added arm needs its own sample and adds comparisons (C12.7). <em>Duration:</em> at least one full weekly cycle, usually longer if behavior takes time to settle (novelty, C14.4). <em>Variance reduction:</em> using each user's behavior before the test to sharpen the comparison can cut the sample needed substantially; a data scientist will set it up, and a PM should know that it exists (C14.7).</p>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> A sized test is a test that can answer the question; many tests are run too small to see the effect they were meant to find. <strong>When not to size at length.</strong> For a quick feasibility check in an interview, the rule of 16 and one sentence on duration are enough.</p>\n<span id=\"c14-c14-4-validity-threats-sample-ratio-mismatch-novelty-and-pri\"></span>\n<h4>C14.4 Validity threats: sample-ratio mismatch, novelty and primacy, interference, contamination, peeking</h4>\n<p>A result is only as good as the test that produced it. FW-C14-03 is a checklist of threats, with a check and a remedy for each.</p>\n<p><strong>Sample-ratio mismatch.</strong> A <strong>sample-ratio mismatch</strong> is a difference between the planned and observed split of units across variants, larger than chance allows, that signals a problem in assignment or logging. Suppose 1,000,000 users are assigned 50/50 and the readout shows 508,000 in control and 492,000 in the variant. The expected count is 500,000 each, and the standard deviation of the count is √(1,000,000 × 0.25) = 500, so the gap of 8,000 is 16 standard deviations: not chance. A smaller gap, 501,000 against 499,000, is 2 standard deviations (p ≈ 0.046), which would occur by chance about once in 22 tests; teams therefore set a stricter alarm (for example p below 0.001) for this check. A real mismatch means something differs between arms in who is counted: a bot filter applied to one arm, a slower variant that loses users before the event is logged, a redirect that fails, an assignment made after exposure. When it fires, the results are untrustworthy, whatever they say. Find the cause, fix it and rerun.</p>\n<p><strong>Novelty and primacy.</strong> The <strong>novelty effect</strong> is a temporary change in behavior because something is new, which fades as the novelty wears off. A new layout can draw extra clicks in the first week because it is different, and the lift shrinks as people get used to it. The mirror image, sometimes called a primacy or change-aversion effect, is a temporary dip because people dislike change before they adapt. The check is to look at the effect over time, by day or week since the user's first exposure, and to compare new users (who have no old habit) with existing users. A lift that decays toward a stable level tells you the long-run effect is the stable level. Run long enough to see it settle, or keep a long-term holdout (C14.6).</p>\n<p><strong>Interference.</strong> <strong>Interference</strong> is when one unit's treatment affects another unit's outcome, so that the control group is not a clean picture of no change. It is common wherever people or sellers interact. In a social product, if treated users send more messages, control users receive them and change their own behavior. In a marketplace, if treated buyers get a discount, they take supply away from control buyers. In both cases the measured difference between arms is not the effect you would see if everyone were treated. Remedies: randomize clusters (whole friend groups, cities, markets) so that interaction stays within an arm; use time-based alternation (switchback) in a marketplace; or measure spillover directly.</p>\n<p><strong>Contamination.</strong> Users who see both variants (two devices, shared accounts, a bug in assignment) blur the comparison and understate any effect. Assign by a stable identifier and check the share of units seen in both arms.</p>\n<p><strong>Peeking.</strong> <strong>Peeking</strong> is checking the results repeatedly during a test and stopping when the difference first looks significant, which inflates false positives. If nothing is going on and you test at the 5% level once, you are wrong 5% of the time. Look at accumulating data at 2 equally spaced points and stop at the first significant one, and the false-positive rate is about 8%; at 5 looks, about 14%; at 10 looks, about 19%; at 20 looks, about 25% (simulated for this chapter, consistent with the sequential-testing literature). The remedies are to fix the sample size and the duration in advance and look once at the end, or to use a sequential method designed for repeated looks, which a data scientist can set up. Watching a dashboard for problems (a guardrail that signals harm) is different from stopping early for success.</p>\n";
